      "tool": "Energy & equilibrium",
      "text": "Haber–Bosch equilibrium (Kp)\n\nN2 + 3 H2 ⇌ 2 NH3 at 400 °C (673 K) with Kp = 1.6e-4 atm⁻² from 50 atm N2 and 150 atm H2: the ICE table gives the partial pressures at equilibrium and the conversion.\n\nKp = 0.00016 atm⁻² (Δn(gas) = -2), initial partial pressures: N2 = 50, H2 = 150, NH3 = 0.\nAt equilibrium: N2 = 26.65 atm, H2 = 79.96 atm, NH3 = 46.69 atm; total 153.3 atm (from 200 atm).\nMole fraction of ammonia 30.46 %; conversion of the limiting reactant 46.69 %; extent ξ = 23.35 atm.\nThe reaction is exothermic (ΔH = −92 kJ/mol): at 773 K, van 't Hoff gives Kp = 1.91e-5, much less ammonia. Compressing the mixture to half its volume (the \"volume\" box) or adding H2 shifts it forward.",
